{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793650202,"nonce":"olk2tZEce8jMDbO476lbbT94yks=","title":"Abandoned fibers (unfinished generators and coroutines) are never reclaimed","message":"A suspended fiber keeps its mmap'd stack (usable range plus a guard page) and its Box\u003cFiber\u003e until it finishes or is freed explicitly, and its stack is an unconditional collector root. A generator or coroutine that is dropped before it finishes is therefore a permanent leak: `for x in gen(): break`, next(it) once, any(...) over a generator, a Lua coroutine.wrap called once.\n\nRepro (Mac release): 100,000 `it = g(i); next(it)` with a two-yield generator: peak RSS 1.5 GB, footprint 1.7 GB, about 15 KB RSS each plus a 64 MiB mapping (Lua) per fiber. Lua coroutine.wrap, one call each, 100,000: peak RSS 1.68 GB, 3.4 GB footprint. Exhausted generators in the same loop: 58 MB.\n\nConsequences on a server: unbounded RSS; each suspended fiber is also scanned by every collection (stack_windows), so later collections slow down (a 400k-yield loop went from 0.5 s to 3.3 s after 100k abandoned coroutines). On Linux each stack is two VMAs, so about 32k abandoned fibers reach vm.max_map_count and trip the `mmap failed for fiber stack` assert (not tested).\n\nDirection: allocate the Fiber in the collector's heap and treat a suspended fiber's stack as reachable only when the fiber itself is (ephemeron), then drop unreached fibers after marking so their stacks return to the system or a stack cache. Related: the cost of creating and finishing a fiber is dominated by mmap, mprotect and munmap (completed Lua coroutine: 9 us each, 0.76 of 0.92 s is system time); a per-thread stack cache in krio-fiber addresses that part.","files":null}]}